WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
<v Mike>Super Spike V-Ball Nintendo World Cup.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:08.240
<v Mike>Bump, Set, Spike, Pass, Shoot, Score!

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:31.680
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every SKU released in North America.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.740
<v Hosts>I'm Mike.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.000
<v Hosts>I'm Sean.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.380
<v Hosts>And I'm Joe.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:42.240
<v Mike>Guys, we had briefly covered in last week's episode that the reason for this existing

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.920
<v Mike>was the same reason as last week's episode, which was to sell Nintendo consoles in various

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:56.620
<v Mike>bundles. This one being, hey, we published two sports games that both have four-player compatibility.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:02.000
<v Mike>Why don't we just sell four controllers and an adapter for those four controllers and an

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.940
<v Mike>NES in this game and see if anybody will bite.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:11.520
<v Mike>And let's do it at the same time as we sell a different bundle that has an NES, two controllers,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.800
<v Mike>a Zapper, and an NES Power Pad, which everybody wants, right?

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:17.800
<v Mike>Everybody wants a Power Pad.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.560
<v Mike>But since we covered that last episode, and there's really not much else to say there unless

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.880
<v Mike>this just, you know, maybe just real fast sound like a deal to you, which one's the better

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.220
<v Joe>deal? Definitely last week's, right?

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.980
<v Joe>I mean, this is great, but I mean, it's like a sport that is not very popular, like not,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.820
<v Joe>you know, not like one of the main popular sports, which is volleyball.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.560
<v Sean>And then soccer, which is pretty popular worldwide.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.380
<v Joe>But it's also like, I don't know, I feel like not everyone's favorite soccer game or anything.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.380
<v Joe>It's just like the other one is like Mario and Duck Hunt.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.920
<v Joe>You know, it's like they're iconic.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:02.520
<v Sean>I'd say that even though they kind of force you to dispose of that last peripheral, like

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.060
<v Sean>it's really just being like, and take this too.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.880
<v Sean>Even though you then have to throw that out and like deal with Garbage Day, it's still

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.160
<v Mike>better value. Or if you're, you know, in Joe's case, you could just use it as a towel.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.040
<v Joe>Yes. Use a towel as it.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.660
<v Mike>Yes. No, no.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.720
<v Mike>Or this time reverse it.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.140
<v Mike>Oh, just use it as your towel.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.480
<v Joe>Use a power pad as your towel. Because all my towels are in use for being power pads.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.000
<v Joe>So I need to use the power pad as a towel.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.820
<v Sean>Yes. I agree.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.820
<v Mike>I think it's a no brainer that even regardless of whether the power pad that comes with it

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.500
<v Mike>works or not, the fact that you're getting Mario Brothers, Super Mario Brothers, and Duck

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.120
<v Mike>Hunt, and a working zapper.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.700
<v Mike>And all that other stuff is just like, it opens up more opportunity for you on the NES

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:52.640
<v Mike>as opposed to this one where I really feel like these were, these were fine games, Super

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.640
<v Mike>Spike V-Ball and Nintendo World Cup.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.640
<v Mike>But I know I personally would not have ordered this one.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.180
<v Mike>I wouldn't have asked for it for Christmas.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:06.760
<v Mike>It would be like, to me, Joe, to your point, these are not the exciting sports I want.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.260
<v Mike>I want a baseball game.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.240
<v Mike>I want a hockey game.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.860
<v Mike>I would settle for a football game.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:14.500
<v Mike>And then eventually if I found out it was Tecmo Bowl, I'd be really happy that it was

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.780
<v Mike>a football game. But yeah, that's like where my head's at.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.520
<v Mike>And that's kind of what I wanted this episode to be about because we are always talking about

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:28.860
<v Mike>since literally the very first episode, which was 10 Yard Fight, we have mentioned that like,

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.980
<v Mike>I don't know what it's going to take for a sports game to be on the essential games list.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:40.020
<v Mike>And rather than say what we think an NES game that could be on the essential games list as a sports game is

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:46.940
<v Mike>or could be or set up rules for it, I just more or less wanted to explore sports video games in general

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.540
<v Mike>that we think are essential games.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:55.620
<v Mike>Games that you, whether they were exposed to you at an early age for whatever console you had

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.200
<v Mike>and you were like, yep, I still think fondly of this game.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.220
<v Mike>Or if it's actually like, yeah, it's still one of my favorite video games,

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:03.740
<v Mike>even though it's a sports game.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.860
<v Mike>That's kind of what I wanted this episode to be, just to maybe color some perspective

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:13.700
<v Mike>on why we don't vote these, historically we don't vote these NES games as essential.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.040
<v Sean>I mean, we have with one, right?

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.320
<v Joe>Yeah, sure.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.480
<v Joe>At least. I think maybe there were two, weren't there?

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.040
<v Mike>No, two. Yeah, there was Baseball Stars as well.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.060
<v Sean>Yes, yes, okay.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.620
<v Sean>But yes, there is a higher bar for these games.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:31.960
<v Mike>And so just thinking about it, a lot of sports games for me that I am,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:42.300
<v Mike>fond of are mostly because I played them at a young age and they had the roster that I enjoyed as a kid.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.740
<v Mike>The superstars I looked up to.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.560
<v Mike>And I don't think those are great criterias for a good video game.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:59.340
<v Mike>And I'm sure if I played some of those today, I would actually prefer maybe some of the more like bare bones gameplay that's happening here on the NES than on those games.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:09.280
<v Mike>Because the early 3D era wasn't exactly, you know, the peak output for any video game genre.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.120
<v Mike>I don't think anybody would say that the best baseball game lives on the N64.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:22.700
<v Sean>Yeah, I think the real turning point was like the PS3 era.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.380
<v Sean>I think that's when people started to see them as degrading in quality.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:38.480
<v Sean>So I would say that PS2, Xbox, those were when most of the great sports games were coming out.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.980
<v Mike>Yeah. And I'll be honest.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:47.980
<v Joe>I think I might be not the best person for this experiment because I was not a big sports game person.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.640
<v Joe>Both of my brothers, big, big into MLB and NHL.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.540
<v Joe>And, you know, I definitely dabbled.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:02.920
<v Joe>But most traditional team sports games, I'm probably not going to vote on the essential games regardless because it's just not generally for me.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.420
<v Joe>But I do have some thoughts.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:14.940
<v Mike>We'll get into it. Yeah, well, I would actually like to start with you then, Joe, just to kind of get a baseline of when you think, you know, greatest sports games that you've played.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.100
<v Mike>Obviously, there's way more available than you've had access to.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.800
<v Mike>But like what comes to mind as your great sports video games?

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:29.000
<v Joe>Well, so, you know, as a kid, it would always be like I'd only be playing these games if it was like my brother or one of my friends.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.100
<v Joe>Like they just needed someone else.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.140
<v Joe>And I'm like, all right, fine.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:34.440
<v Joe>I'll play, you know, NHL or I'll play MLB.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:35.360
<v Joe>I mean, those are the big ones.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.640
<v Joe>I never really played Madden much or anything like that.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:43.300
<v Joe>But but those like are the ones that I would play here and there.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:52.320
<v Joe>I remember also playing a Ken Griffey game where you could play like on the kitchen table and you'd like the home run was like hitting it over the milk and stuff like that.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:53.840
<v Joe>That was kind of fun.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:07:00.340
<v Joe>But I think for me, it's more like if you want me to talk about standout sports games that I would call essential.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.580
<v Joe>They're not team sports games.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.140
<v Joe>They're Tony Hawk's Pro Skater or SSX Tricky, things like that.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:12.580
<v Joe>I mean, I spent tons of time in Tony Hawk and especially the first one when the remake came out more recently like that.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:13.840
<v Joe>That game holds up.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:19.580
<v Joe>And it is I think for me what it is, is that it's something that I can't do.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:25.560
<v Joe>You know, I could get on a skateboard and go a couple feet, but like I cannot really do anything that's happening in that game.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:35.460
<v Joe>And like obviously, if I'm playing an NHL game, I can't play hockey like the people in that game, but I could like still play amateur, you know, backyard hockey.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.840
<v Joe>Whereas I just like can't even do anything on a skateboard.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:45.320
<v Joe>So it's like a little more wish fulfillment or a little more like amazing to me that I can like I think that's where the fun comes in.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:51.280
<v Joe>Whereas like I can do all this stuff that like I'd have to work my whole life to be able to even come close to doing in reality.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:54.120
<v Mike>And I totally count those as sports games.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:08:02.620
<v Mike>Like when I'm thinking about this, I would include ESPN NFL 2K5 as the same sports game as Mario Golf.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.460
<v Mike>You know, like I don't achieving two different things, but they're definitely both sports video games.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:16.400
<v Sean>Yeah, but like I think like there and I don't want to call them sub genres, but I think that they are like Tony Hawk.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:29.260
<v Sean>Yes, skateboarding is a sport in a way, but it's not a sports game to me, nor is anything that Mario does.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:41.740
<v Sean>If Mario got onto the gridiron and was like the quarterback of the Mushroom Kingdom mushrooms or something, then maybe.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.140
<v Sean>But I don't know.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.760
<v Mike>So there was a Mario baseball game.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:50.620
<v Mike>Are you saying that that is falling on the line?

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:52.720
<v Mike>Yeah, I guess it depends.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.600
<v Sean>I've never seen it. So maybe tennis.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:09:00.440
<v Mike>There was also a Mario soccer game. Like golf games are like, I guess those are sports games.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:05.460
<v Joe>I guess it's just whether or not you're considering it a team sport game or just sports games in general.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:07.240
<v Joe>Like I feel like I get what you're saying.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.860
<v Joe>I feel like the games that I normally think of when I think of sports games are team sports games.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.060
<v Joe>Hockey, baseball, football, soccer.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:15.420
<v Sean>You know, things like that.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.340
<v Mike>Yeah, I guess I can see it both ways.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.580
<v Sean>It's just a different type of sport. Yeah.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:30.240
<v Mike>What I'm thinking is that like on the NES, we're considering all these games as sports games, whether they take golf in the simulation realm or they try to be, you know, have fun with it.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:37.660
<v Mike>Right. I don't know if we're calling Lunar Pool a sports game, but maybe we should look into that.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:56.640
<v Mike>However, I guess that's what I'm saying, too, is like, you know, I totally understand that Mario Golf is not Tiger Woods PGA Tour, but they both, you know, are like serving a similar function that like I don't think people are playing Mario Golf strictly because they love Mario.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.900
<v Mike>Like you have to have some interest in golf, too.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:33.940
<v Sean>Yeah. That like Dota or League of Legends.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:38.260
<v Sean>Like these are video games with characters and abilities.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:43.240
<v Sean>And it just so happens that people play them competitively.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:50.600
<v Sean>I don't know if I would say that that's a sport, but now we're getting into actual like linguistics and stuff.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:56.680
<v Joe>Yeah. Right. I kind of want to like just for the heck of it workshop that line, like where did I draw that line?

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:04.160
<v Joe>Because I agree with you that like League of Legends or something like definitely not a sport, even if it's an e-sport or if it's a competitive video game.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:10.700
<v Joe>But like Rocket League, that does fall a little bit on like a line because it's like closer.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.140
<v Joe>It's not a real sport.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.520
<v Joe>Yeah. But it's like trying to be like a sport.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.540
<v Joe>You know what I mean? It's like a game about like a hybrid sport.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:29.720
<v Joe>But it is like you're trying to score a goal on another person, you know, like because then I wonder like if if there's there's the baseball game where you have like you can like turn into fire and charge the mound and stuff.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:31.520
<v Joe>And like, is that a sport?

00:11:31.780 --> 00:11:33.060
<v Joe>Oh, it's like baseball. Yes.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.960
<v Joe>Yeah. Like it's baseball, but also there's like you have powers and stuff like that.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.140
<v Joe>So it's like it's weird.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.140
<v Joe>I don't know exactly where it's like very fluid because I don't know exactly where I would draw the line.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:43.680
<v Joe>Like, no, that's just strictly an e-sport.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.620
<v Joe>And this is a sports game.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:52.020
<v Joe>But like, yeah, do we think it has to be based on an actual real life sport or can there be a fictional sport?

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.160
<v Joe>Is Quidditch World Cup a sports game?

00:11:54.540 --> 00:12:04.860
<v Mike>Or like, you know, that's what I was going to say is like, you know, I think I draw the line on it not being a real sport because, Joe, even your that game that we loved that's like the kickball game.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:06.860
<v Joe>Oh, yeah. Kung fu kickball.

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:10.020
<v Mike>Like, that's not a sports game, right?

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:13.620
<v Joe>Yeah. I mean, I wouldn't think so until now that you've challenged me on that.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:17.220
<v Joe>I'm like, well, I guess based on what I just said, it would have to be.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:21.020
<v Mike>So I don't know. It can't be because then like Smash Brothers becomes a sports game.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:26.280
<v Mike>Like, you know, I would even go as far as to say like Gran Turismo games aren't sports games.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:31.360
<v Mike>Those are racing games and they're closer to like need for speed than they are to NFL Blitz.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:33.960
<v Joe>Yeah. I mean, I guess the yeah.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:36.000
<v Joe>And this is a loose line, even as I'm thinking of it.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:46.240
<v Joe>But like the difference between like Smash Brothers and Kung Fu kickball is that Kung Fu kickball is two people with a soccer ball trying to quote unquote score a goal on each other.

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:53.920
<v Joe>Whereas which is like very much seems like it's taken mostly from soccer with like a bunch of other stuff added to it.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:13:00.120
<v Joe>Whereas Super Smash Brothers is like here are these video game characters doing the things they do in their video games and they're all fighting each other.

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:08.220
<v Sean>I propose that the that we go with the idea that the sport has to exist in real life for it to be a sports game.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.900
<v Sean>So NASCAR, those are sports games.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:13.320
<v Joe>Motorsport, I guess, counts.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:24.280
<v Sean>And NFL Blitz is a sports game, even though they don't they they may like, you know, turn on fire or whatever happens.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:25.500
<v Sean>It's been a while.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:27.760
<v Mike>They do. They do turn on fire.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.420
<v Sean>Turn on fire.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:34.560
<v Sean>But it's still based on football.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:43.020
<v Sean>And but I don't think that like Rocket League, even though it's based on soccer, you are not a car in soccer.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.420
<v Joe>So I accept your proposal.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:48.080
<v Mike>I think Joe gave us a good tangent there.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:14:01.320
<v Mike>And now we can go back to the actual question and maybe get an answer from Sean this time around on like, Sean, just some greatest of all time sports video games that are captured in your mind.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:09.920
<v Sean>Yeah. In order, I'll say NBA Jam when I was truly little.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:12.600
<v Mike>Got to be on got to be on the list.

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:17.460
<v Sean>Yeah. So that would be when I had the Super NES played that a lot.

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:24.060
<v Sean>Then it was probably like I think it was NASCAR 98, though.

